## Product Decisions

### PDR-001 — Family account model (not individual accounts)
**Decision:** The top-level entity is a `family_account`, not a user. Multiple parents share one family account. Guests are linked to a family account, not individual parents.
**Reasoning:** Tales belong to the family, not to an individual parent. Both parents should see the same feed, the same guests, and have equal access. Separate accounts with "sharing" would create unnecessary complexity and a worse UX.
**Trade-off:** A parent cannot be a member of two family accounts in v1 (edge case: blended families). This is noted as a future enhancement.

### PDR-002 — Free tier: 25 total tales (not monthly limit)
**Decision:** The free tier caps at 25 total tales stored, not a monthly posting limit.
**Reasoning:** A monthly limit punishes active users and creates anxiety around posting. A total cap creates a single natural upgrade moment — "your library is full" — which is emotionally resonant for a memory app. It also directly ties to our AI cost (each tale = one AI call).
**Upgrade prompt trigger:** Warning banner at 20 tales, hard block at 25.

### PDR-003 — Guests: 2 free, unlimited paid
**Decision:** Free accounts can have up to 2 active guests. Paid accounts have unlimited guests.
**Reasoning:** Guests are the primary viral acquisition channel — completely blocking them on free kills word-of-mouth. 2 guests covers the most common use case (one set of grandparents). "Invite the whole extended family" is a natural and emotionally compelling upgrade driver.

### PDR-004 — AI produces a polished narrative (not a transcript)
**Decision:** The AI pipeline rewrites the parent's recording as a polished 2-4 paragraph narrative. The raw transcript is not shown to the user.
**Reasoning:** The core product value is turning a rushed, rambling voice note into something beautiful enough to share and keep. Showing a cleaned-up transcript would be half the magic. The parent can still edit the narrative freely after AI generates it.
**Constraint:** The AI must not invent facts or change the meaning of what was said. This is enforced in the system prompt.

### PDR-005 — Inspiration prompts: generic for v1
**Decision:** Home screen inspiration prompts are a hardcoded rotating list of ~20 generic prompts in v1. Personalised prompts (based on child age and tale history) are deferred.
**Reasoning:** Personalised prompts require ML infrastructure and a reasonable tale history to learn from. Generic prompts deliver 80% of the value for 5% of the build effort. This is an explicit v1 scope decision, not an oversight.

### PDR-006 — Printed book: placeholder flow for v1 launch
**Decision:** The printed book feature launches with a "notify me" email capture placeholder rather than a full print partner integration.
**Reasoning:** Print partner contracts (Momento, Chatbooks, etc.) take time to negotiate. The PDF generation (TICKET-030) is built and real — only the ordering step is a placeholder. This lets us validate demand before committing to an integration.

### PDR-007 — No tab navigation
**Decision:** The app uses stack-based navigation with no tab bar. The only persistent navigation element is the FAB for adding a new tale.
**Reasoning:** Explicit product decision from the design phase. The app is intentionally simple — Home → Tale Detail is the primary flow. Account and settings are secondary. A tab bar would give equal visual weight to settings, which competes with the core action.

